      "title": "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2ddd3933-72c1-5065-a585-240672cd2b75",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=12 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-2)=-6.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-6-(-2)=-4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=-4$ and ${x}_{3}=-2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(-4)-(-2)=12.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=12-(4)-(2)=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=6$, ${x}_{2}=-4$, and ${x}_{3}=-2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨6,-4,-2⟩.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
